<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">verified_user</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Access Control</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            Access control works at two levels: the entity declares its role in authorization, and each
            property declares whether it is readable or writable, by which roles, and for which records.
        </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Entity roles</h2>
    <p>
        Authorization starts with the entities. In an entity's <strong>Entity Identity</strong> section, the
        <strong>Type</strong> assigns its role in the authorization model:
    </p>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Authorizable</strong> &mdash; the entity that authenticates: the account a request is made as.</li>
        <li><strong>Authorizable Role</strong> &mdash; the entity that carries roles, such as Moderator, Editor, or Viewer.</li>
        <li><strong>Authorization</strong> &mdash; the record that links an authorizable to its roles.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        These entity-level choices are what give the per-property roles and scopes below their meaning. Set
        them first &mdash; see <a class="help-link" href="#entities" data-help-anchor="entities">Working with Entities</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Property controls</h2>
    <p>
        Select an attribute or relationship to manage its access controls. Add <strong>#Readable</strong>
        or <strong>#Writable</strong> entries from the access control toolbar, enable or disable them,
        and choose the scope.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Roles and scopes</h2>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Roles</strong> &mdash; add built-in roles such as Moderator, Editor, and Viewer, or create a custom role.</li>
        <li><strong>All</strong> &mdash; allows the role to access matching properties across all records.</li>
        <li><strong>Own</strong> &mdash; limits access to records owned by the current user or authorization context.</li>
        <li><strong>Owner relationships</strong> &mdash; mark the relationship that identifies ownership for generated access checks.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">lock</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Access control starts in the model.</strong>
            The generated service uses these declarations to decide which fields can be read or written
            by each role and scope.
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
